<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP: PERMITTED, and deliberately so. Full reasoning, because
    "allow cleartext globally" normally deserves a fight.

    The design (design spec "Sign in > Unencrypted-server sheet") requires that a
    plain-`http://` provider still works after the user explicitly accepts the
    risk: the sheet's "Continue anyway" button signs in over http, and that
    path must genuinely connect. The obvious defence — a per-host allow-list
    in this file — is impossible: this XML is compiled into the APK and the
    platform never re-reads it at runtime, and NetworkSecurityPolicy has NO
    public setter for cleartext (an earlier plan to call
    setCleartextTrafficPermitted(host, true) after the sheet was based on an
    API that does not exist publicly). A static file cannot know
    `provider.example` before the user types it.

    So the per-host gate lives app-side, where it can see the user's answer:

      - LoginViewModel.attempt() sends nothing over `http://` until
        CleartextConsentStore holds an answer for that hostname. The first
        Sign-in raises the sheet; the answer is remembered per host.
        "Never skip this sheet silently" is enforced by unit tests
        (LoginViewModelTest.submit_httpUrl_neverAnswered_raisesSheet_*).
      - The sheet is the only writer of consent.

    Trust anchors stay system-only below, so cleartext-permitted does NOT also
    mean "trust user-installed CAs", and TLS traffic is unaffected by this
    flag. If a future build ever needs the stricter posture (e.g. a global
    "block cleartext" switch in Settings), flip base-config back to false and
    decide then what http-only providers get — with the sheet in mind.

    On API 23 this file is ignored entirely and cleartext is allowed anyway,
    so v1's posture matches the oldest supported device already.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- Deliberate: the sheet is the per-host gate, see comment above. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
